<Layout
	title="Affiliate Disclosure"
	description="How WeirdJapan.news uses affiliate links, who pays us, and what that means for our editorial. Required notices for Amazon, Booking.com, Klook, and other partner programs."
	pubDate={new Date('2026-05-24')}
	category="About"
>
	<p>
		<strong>WeirdJapan.news participates in affiliate marketing programs.</strong> When you click a link
		on this site that points to a retailer, hotel-booking platform, tour operator, or other merchant
		and you subsequently make a purchase, we may receive a commission &mdash; at no additional cost
		to you.
	</p>
	<p>
		This page documents which programs we belong to, the disclosure language each program requires,
		and the editorial principles that govern how we use affiliate links. It is here so that readers
		know exactly where we stand. We follow the U.S. Federal Trade Commission's
		<a href="https://www.ftc.gov/business-guidance/resources/disclosures-101-social-media-influencers" target="_blank" rel="noopener nofollow">disclosure
		guidance</a> (16 CFR &sect;255), the Amazon Associates Operating Agreement, and the equivalent
		requirements of each affiliate program we join.
	</p>

	<h2>Amazon Associates &mdash; required notice</h2>
	<p>
		<strong>As an Amazon Associate, WeirdJapan.news earns from qualifying purchases.</strong>
	</p>
	<p>
		We are participants in the Amazon Services LLC Associates Program and the Amazon Japan
		Associates Program. These are affiliate advertising programs designed to provide a means for
		sites to earn advertising fees by linking to Amazon.com and Amazon.co.jp. International
		visitors are automatically routed to their regional Amazon storefront via Amazon OneLink where
		applicable. Prices, availability, and product listings are determined by Amazon, not by
		WeirdJapan.news.
	</p>

	<h2>Hotel and travel partners</h2>
	<p>
		We participate in the following travel-industry affiliate programs:
	</p>
	<ul>
		<li><strong>Booking.com</strong> &mdash; via the Awin affiliate network. Commissions are paid
			when a referred reservation completes its stay.</li>
		<li><strong>Klook</strong> &mdash; via the Klook Affiliate Program. Commissions are paid on
			completed tour or activity bookings.</li>
		<li><strong>Agoda, Expedia, GetYourGuide, Viator</strong> &mdash; we may add these in future as
			coverage warrants. Any addition will be reflected on this page first.</li>
	</ul>
	<p>
		Booking.com, Klook, and other travel partners set their own prices. Booking through our links
		does not change your price; we simply receive a small referral commission from the partner if
		your reservation completes.
	</p>

	<h2>Japanese affiliate networks</h2>
	<p>
		We work with the following Japan-based affiliate service providers (ASPs):
	</p>
	<ul>
		<li><strong>A8.net</strong> &mdash; for partnerships with Japan-based merchants and services.</li>
		<li><strong>Moshimo Affiliate (&#x3082;&#x3057;&#x3082;&#x30a2;&#x30d5;&#x30a3;&#x30ea;&#x30a8;&#x30a4;&#x30c8;)</strong>
			&mdash; for certain merchant relationships within Japan.</li>
		<li><strong>Rakuten Affiliate</strong> &mdash; for product and travel links via the Rakuten
			marketplace.</li>
	</ul>

	<h2>How we use affiliate links</h2>
	<p>
		Our editorial rules around affiliate links:
	</p>
	<ul>
		<li>
			<strong>We do not add affiliate links to recommendations we would not otherwise make.</strong>
			Editorial selection happens first. Affiliate links are added afterward to recommendations
			that already passed our editorial filter.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>We do not accept payment for editorial coverage.</strong> No brand, hotel, tourism
			board, or other entity can buy their way into our articles. Sponsored content, if we ever
			run it, will be clearly labeled as such and will not be commingled with editorial.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>We disclose at the link.</strong> Where required by the FTC or by an affiliate
			program's terms, we annotate individual links or article sections with a brief disclosure
			that the link is an affiliate link.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Article quality and accuracy are not influenced by commission rates.</strong>
			We will favorably review a competitor with a 1% commission rate over an inferior partner
			with an 8% commission rate. Every time.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>We do not use cookie-stuffing, link-cloaking deception, or any other technique
			that violates our partners' terms of service</strong> or that would mislead readers about
			what they are clicking.
		</li>
	</ul>

	<h2>AI-assisted content disclosure</h2>
	<p>
		WeirdJapan.news is produced with AI-assisted research, drafting, and editing. Every article
		passes through a multi-stage human-reviewable editorial process (Writer/Evaluator rounds)
		before publication. We do not fabricate first-person experiences. When an article appears to
		describe a personal visit, that wording reflects editorial framing of researched material, not
		a literal personal trip by the author. Where this distinction matters &mdash; restaurant
		reviews, hotel stays, transportation experiences &mdash; we lean toward observational language
		("Travelers report&hellip;", "The hotel is&hellip;") rather than fabricated first-person
		claims.
	</p>
	<p>
		If you spot an inaccuracy in any article, please email
		<a href="mailto:hi@weirdjapan.news">hi@weirdjapan.news</a> and we will correct it promptly.
	</p>

	<h2>Cookies and tracking</h2>
	<p>
		Most affiliate links place a cookie on your browser when you click. This cookie identifies
		WeirdJapan.news as the referring site so we can be credited for any subsequent purchase. We do
		not have access to your personal information, browsing history, or purchase data &mdash; the
		retailer or platform manages all of that on their side. Cookie lifetimes vary by program
		(typically 24 hours for Amazon, 30 days for many travel affiliates).
	</p>
	<p>
		We do not currently use third-party advertising networks or behavioral re-targeting pixels on
		this site. If we ever add them, this page will reflect it before they go live.
	</p>

	<h2>Contact</h2>
	<p>
		Questions about this disclosure, our editorial practices, or a specific article? Email
		<a href="mailto:hi@weirdjapan.news">hi@weirdjapan.news</a>. We read everything.
	</p>

	<p style="margin-top: 3em; font-size: 0.85rem; color: #777;">
		Last updated: 2026-05-24.
	</p>
</Layout>
